{{History of Balisca}}

The '''Special Period''' ({{Langx|bl|Período especial}}), also known as the '''Adjustment Period''' ({{Langx|bl|Período adequación|links=no}}), was an extended period of [[October 76|political]], {{W|food crisis|food}}, {{W|energy crisis|energy}} and {{W|economic crisis}} in [[Balisca]] that began in late 1976, primarily due the [[Camboriú War]] with [[Potocsí]], the [[Bight of Ayorin Crisis]], and the onset of the {{W|Adonian oil crisis|1980s oil crisis}}. The {{W|economic depression}} of the Special Period was at its most severe between 1977 and 1983, coinciding with the oil shock and [[Marañón oil fires]] and the collapse of the [[Petroleum industry in Balisca|Baliscan petroleum industry]], but improved thereafter as a result of societal changes and assistance from [[Burawa]], [[Daras]] and members of the [[Socialist Bloc]], whom together transported over 2.8 million {{W|tonnes}} of food and other materials to Balisca.

The worst of the crisis coincided with the election of [[Ecological Action|ecologist]] [[Otávia Brito]] to the position of [[Secretary General of Balisca|Secretary General]]. Under her leadership, the government encouraged and later assisted citizens in growing their own food on any available pieces of land. Urban gardens sprang up throughout the country on roof-tops, patios, and unused parking lots in raised beds as well as "squatting" on empty lots. These efforts were furthered by the [[Secretariat of Local Administration and Modernization (Balisca)|Secretariat of Local Development and Modernization]]'s efforts to teach {{W|permaculture}}, a sustainable agricultural system, and supporting {{W|communal farming}} through {{W|agricultural cooperative}}s, fostering a sense of {{W|communalism|communalism}} of community engagement and {{W|capacity building}}.

Changes during the Special Period included an unprecedented level of responsibility and cooperation at the [[Politics of Balisca|confederal level]] by the [[Communes of Balisca|communes]] and [[Regions of Balisca|regions]], and internationally, the diplomatic realignment of the country towards [[Burawa]] with the [[Mutual Arms and Technology Agreement]]. This period radically transformed Baliscan society and the economy, as it necessitated the revival of [[Rural society in Balisca|rural Balisca]], a return to {{W|organic agriculture}}, curtailed {{W|meat consumption}}, stunted the growth of [[W:Car ownership|car ownership]] in the country (fuel was prioritized for {{W|diesel locomotive|diesel}} cargo trains), and overhauled domestic industry, health and diets.

Despite the struggles, Balisca made significant strides in reducing dependency on foreign sources for fuel through {{W|ethanol production}}, increased {{W|energy efficiency}}, {{W|renewable energy}} proliferation, and was able to secure new food imports long enough to implemented measures to increase domestic food production. 

== Background ==

{{main|Camboriú War|Bight of Ayorin Crisis|Marañón oil fires}}

The Special Period emerged from the convergence of multiple crises in the mid-1970s: [[Camboriú War]] (1976–1979) with [[Potocsí]] disrupted regional trade and required massive resource mobilization. Simultaneously, the [[Bight of Ayorin Crisis]] saw [[Ayorin]] attacks on [[Sanyan]] that interrupted additional oil imports and threatened maritime supply lines with Kaftia. The [[1980s oil crisis|global oil shock]] compounded these pressures, while domestic disaster struck with the [[Marañón oil fires]]—catastrophic well fires and spills intentionally started by [[Potocsí|Potocsino forces]] in the [[Estuaria]]-[[Pindorama]] oil basin that devastated production capacity and caused severe environmental damage.

The destruction of domestic petroleum infrastructure proved permanent. The Marañón oil field complex, which had supplied approximately 80% of Balisca's domestic petroleum needs, was rendered inoperable by the combination of wartime damage, fire, and spill contamination. Recovery efforts faced not only technical challenges but also sustained opposition from the affected communities and militant environmental movements.

== Impact by region ==

The oil-producing regions of [[Estuaria]] and [[Pindorama]] experienced the most severe and lasting damage. The Marañón oil fires burned for over eighteen months before being fully extinguished, releasing an estimated 45 million barrels of oil into the environment—contaminating groundwater, agricultural land, and coastal fisheries. In Pindorama, the oil disaster intersected with [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|indigenous]] sovereignty issues, as the fields had been developed on indigenous territories under contentious arrangements. The fires and spills validated long-standing indigenous critiques of the petroleum industry, transforming previously disputed extraction into widely condemned contamination.

Urban populations faced acute food shortages and transportation disruption. The rationing of fuel for private vehicles effectively ended the growth of [[W:Car ownership|car ownership]] that had begun in the 1960s. Public transit systems were prioritized but initially overwhelmed by the surge in demand. The crisis catalyzed rapid innovation in [[W:Urban agriculture|urban agriculture]], [[W:Micromobility|micromobility]], and community-based food distribution networks. [[Rural society in Balisca|Rural Balisca]] experienced a reversal of decades of [[W:Urbanization|urbanization]] trends. With food supply chains disrupted and urban employment contracted, significant population flows returned to rural communes. This "rural revival" accelerated the [[W:Organic agriculture|organic agriculture]] transition (''[[W:Organopónicos|Organopónicos]]'') and revitalized cooperative farming traditions that had been declining since the 1950s.

== Aftermath and legacy ==

=== Permanent collapse of the petroleum industry ===

The Baliscan petroleum industry never recovered from the Special Period. While initial projections suggested that oil production could resume within a decade of the Marañón disaster, multiple factors prevented restoration:

* The extent of environmental contamination rendered significant portions of the oil field infrastructure unusable
* Technical expertise and investment capital shifted toward [[W:Renewable energy|renewable energy]] and [[W:Ethanol production|ethanol production]]
* [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Indigenous communities]] and [[Ecological Action|environmental movements]] organized sustained opposition to any revival

Between 1984 and 1991, seventeen attempts to restart oil production were documented across Estuaria and Pindorama. Each attempt faced protests, work stoppages, and in several cases, direct sabotage of equipment and infrastructure. Radical ecologists and indigenous militants formed clandestine networks targeting oil infrastructure, with the most prominent being the [[Ecological Resistance Front]] and the [[W:Indigenism|indigenist]] [[Children of Tupã]]. These groups claimed responsibility for at least 23 documented attacks on oil facilities between 1985 and 1992, including pipeline bombings, wellhead sabotages, and equipment destruction.

The [[Confederal Congress]] formally ended petroleum exploration and extraction permits in 1993 through the [[Petroleum Phase-Out Initiative]], which designated all former oil-producing zones as [[W:Ecological restoration|ecological restoration]] areas under indigenous co-management. The act represented the final legislative acknowledgment that the Baliscan petroleum industry was definitively closed.

=== Indigenous and environmental legacy ===

The oil disaster transformed Baliscan attitudes toward extractive industry. Public opinion polling from the period shows a collapse in support for petroleum production, from 62% supporting continued extraction in 1975 to just 8% by 1984.

In Pindorama, the disaster accelerated the expansion of indigenous sovereignty. The contamination of traditional territories became a central grievance in negotiations with the confederal government, resulting in the [[1986 Arapongas Accords]] that transferred former oil lands to indigenous management and established the precedent for ecological co-governance arrangements now common across Balisca.

The radical environmental movement that emerged during this period—including both legal organizations like [[Ecological Action]] and clandestine groups engaged in [[W:Ecotage|direct action]]—shaped Baliscan environmental politics for subsequent decades. The tactic of infrastructure sabotage, though controversial, entered the Baliscan political lexicon as "defensive ecology" (''ecolóxica'' ''defensiva''), framed by proponents as legitimate resistance against ecocidal activity rather than mere property destruction.

=== Low Impact Initiative and energy transition ===

{{main|Low Impact Initiative}}

The Special Period catalyzed Balisca's definitive pivot away from fossil fuels. The [[Low Impact Initiative]] (''Iniciativa Baixo Impacto'', '''IBI'''), first proposed in 1977, formalized this shift into comprehensive urban planning, transportation, and energy policy. The IBI rejected [[W:Car dependency|car-centric development]] in favor of [[W:Micromobility|micromobility]], [[W:Public transit|public transit]], and [[W:Human-centered design|human-centered infrastructure]].

Under the IBI framework, Balisca developed:
* The [[Confederal Electric Mobility Initiative]] (IMEC), funding [[W:Multimobility|electric microvehicle]] development
* Comprehensive [[W:Rail transport|rail electrification]] programs
* Urban planning mandates ensuring no resident was more than 500 meters from public transit
* The world's largest [[W:Permaculture|permaculture]] and urban agriculture network

The energy transition accelerated during the 1980s with significant investment in [[W:Wind power|wind]], [[W:Solar energy|solar]], [[W:Hydroelectricity|hydroelectric]], and eventually [[W:Nuclear power|nuclear]] generation. The [[Araucaria]] nuclear program, developed with [[Burawa|Burawi]] assistance, came online in 1989 and expanded substantially through the 1990s. By 2000, Balisca had achieved complete energy independence without petroleum.

=== Agricultural transformation ===

The food crisis spurred a fundamental restructuring of Baliscan agriculture. The shift toward [[W:Organic agriculture|organic agriculture]], begun during wartime necessity, became permanent policy. Meat consumption declined substantially as resource-intensive livestock production gave way to plant-based protein sources. Urban agriculture, initially a survival measure, became institutionalized through cooperative networks and communal land allocation for food production. The Special Period also accelerated the development of [[W:Ethanol fuel|ethanol fuel]] from [[W:Sugarcane|sugarcane]] and other crops, reducing dependence on imported petroleum and providing an agricultural outlet that supported rural cooperative economies.

=== Cultural and social legacy ===

The Special Period remains a defining event in Baliscan collective memory, frequently referenced in political discourse as evidence of Balisca's capacity for collective action and transformation. The period is credited with:
* Establishing [[W:Resilience (social-ecological)|resilience]] as a core Baliscan value
* Reviving [[W:Communalism|communalism]] and cooperative economics as lived practice rather than mere ideology
* Cementing [[W:Environmental sustainability|environmental sustainability]] as non-partisan consensus
* Demonstrating the viability of [[W:Degrowth|degrowth]]-compatible prosperity

Critics argue that the Special Period also produced lasting anxieties about scarcity that continue to shape Baliscan politics, contributing to risk-averse economic management and suspicion of consumption-oriented policies. The [[Reformist Bloc (Balisca)|Reformist Bloc]]'s eventual collapse during the 2021 unrest was partially attributed to its association with pre-Special Period consumerist aspirations.

